republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/two-senses: P2 (S3): "wise" is taken as shrewd and clever at getting the advantage, as Thrasymachus calls the unjust wise in P2 P3 and as having a craft's knowledge, as the musician or physician is wise where wise is used else, a reader says: the argument does not turn on it
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/two-senses: P2 (S3): "good" is taken as successful and well-off through getting the advantage in P2 P3 and as good at one's craft insofar as one knows it where good is used else, a reader says: the argument does not turn on it
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/equivocates: P5 (S27): "more" is taken as seeks to outdo others like oneself in performing a craft, e.g. tuning strings or prescribing in P5 P6 and as seeks to get the advantage over or have more than others like oneself where overlike is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C1 (S35) does not follow (republic-overreach.logic.r1.sense3)
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C1 (S35): D = {another, fool, just, sage, unjust}; good = {just}, knowing = {just}, overlike = {just}, overlike_2 = ∅, overunlike = {just}, wise = {just}
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C2 (S37): D = {another, fool, just, sage, unjust}; bad = {fool}, ignorant = {fool}, overlike = ∅, overlike_2 = {fool}, overunlike = {fool}
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/equivocates: P5 (S27): "more" is taken as seeks to outdo those unlike oneself in performing a craft in P5 P6 and as seeks to get the advantage over or have more than those unlike oneself where overunlike is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C1 (S35) does not follow (republic-overreach.logic.r1.sense4)
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C1 (S35): D = {another, fool, just, sage, unjust}; good = {sage}, knowing = {sage}, overlike = ∅, overunlike = ∅, overunlike_2 = {sage}, wise = {sage}
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C2 (S37): D = {another, fool, just, sage, unjust}; bad = {fool}, ignorant = {fool}, overlike = {fool}, overunlike = ∅, overunlike_2 = {fool}
republic-overreach.logic.r1: argue/two-senses: A2: "like" is taken as resembling in one respect only, here sharing a pattern of what one desires to outdo in A2 C and as of the same nature or character, so that each is such as his like is where like is used else, a reader says: the argument does not turn on it
